#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de671ee-7bba-54ce-bec2-d2d6f5569f4a" :knowledge-point/title "Writing First-Order Linear ODEs in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf6a7f40-b2a6-5fd4-9b0f-2e5200133a8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295537} {:answer/id #uuid "a7a5b571-7dc4-55fc-95b4-794b2cf751c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295538} {:answer/id #uuid "b73c2a1a-ae93-5d95-a7f2-5a426699abed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295539} {:answer/id #uuid "2269ae32-16f1-57f5-940e-8b5cbf1d092a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=(\\operatorname{sec}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295540} {:answer/id #uuid "982f6313-1aff-5013-864c-7f870615840d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b73c2a1a-ae93-5d95-a7f2-5a426699abed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295539} :answer-field/id #uuid "7642a827-a0ad-568b-ba36-f985cbc48b0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295536}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1de67dfe-a866-57fb-b3b3-9dacfc2c3c42" :question/math-academy-id "q-103354" :question/problem "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y=0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,y & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})y & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)y & =-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)}_{⏟}}_{P(x)}y & ={{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}_{⏟}}_{Q(x)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and $Q(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc50a680-dbf8-5d45-a8c3-72c3c15243f6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying First-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002576cf-2ed9-5b4b-85f5-930244d59605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}+3x}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295614} {:answer/id #uuid "0d0692bd-bd42-52cf-a2ae-91371e3aa073" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{y-x}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295615} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7b52c0-c9b5-553d-82dc-5e67fbc14823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295616} {:answer/id #uuid "6da0902d-c64e-5abc-b9d3-c24e178f8591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295617} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a1ecc5-ec11-54dc-a466-7ca7e0f11cdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6da0902d-c64e-5abc-b9d3-c24e178f8591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295617} :answer-field/id #uuid "10b53c59-d11e-5e30-896d-6442479255cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295613}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "438bd143-91da-5d1a-8869-3ed754e59004" :question/math-academy-id "q-79754" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "To determine which of the following differential equations is a first-order linear ordinary differential equation, we attempt to write each option in the standard form.\n\nA first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nLet's attempt to write $(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}$ in the standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{1}{y})\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y(\\frac{2y+{x}^{2}}{y}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2y+{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is in standard form with  $P(x)=-2$ and $Q(x)={x}^{2}.$ Since the equation is written in standard form, we conclude that it is a first-order linear ODE.\n\nAll of the other equations cannot be written in the standard form, and therefore they are not first-order linear ODEs."}]}
